items.text:
`modality="container"` makes the dialog modal within one region of the page, such as a media player, instead of the whole document. It uses the `container` property as its boundary:

```html
<tp-dialog modality="container" label="Session expired">…</tp-dialog>
<script type="module">
  document.querySelector('tp-dialog').container = document.querySelector('#editor-region');
</script>
```

While open, the dialog:

- renders inside the container instead of the native top layer, so it is clipped to the container and stays visible while the container is fullscreen;
- makes only the container's other content inert; content outside the container stays interactive;
- traps Tab focus within the dialog; like document modality it never pulls back focus that moves elsewhere, so overlays another owner adds (such as autofill menus) stay usable;
- does not lock page scroll;
- sizes its Overlay to the container and centers and bounds Content within it;
- handles Escape and close requests only while focus is inside the container, and uses the usual initial and final focus. Final focus is not restored when the user has already moved focus outside the container;
- exposes `aria-modal="false"`, because the rest of the document remains available. The container's inert siblings carry the isolation.

The container must establish the positioning context (`position: relative` or another positioned value). The layer is placed at the top of the container's stacking context, so the container should also establish one (`isolation: isolate`). Outside presses within the container hit the Overlay and follow `closeOnOutsideInteraction`; presses outside the container never dismiss.

Without a usable container (none, an unresolved ref or resolver, another document, or a failed portal mount), the dialog uses document `modal` and reports a `dialog-modality-container` diagnostic while open. Alert Dialog supports `modal` and `container`. Drawer and Side Panel use viewport geometry, so they do not support `container`: it normalizes to `modal` and reports a `drawer-modality-unsupported` diagnostic.

The Storybook controls do not offer `container`; the media player's error dialog shows a working composition.
